Because the environment states "repositories and compute are not required," the design must reference the published InfLLM recipe as the source of ground truth and include a **pre-registration step** to confirm architectural details from the paper/code before finalizing.

## 2) Proposed approach — design skeleton (design sketch only, nothing executed)

**Goal statement.** Decompose InfLLM's long-context performance into (A) the contribution of *dynamic* memory lookup vs. static/heuristic block selection, and (B) the contribution of *unit representation quality* vs. degenerate representations.

**Ablation conditions (factorial, 2 × 3 core grid):**

| Factor | Levels |
|---|---|
| **Memory lookup** | (A1) Dynamic learned retrieval (original InfLLM) — reference; (A2) Fixed/heuristic selection (e.g., recency + deterministic stride/position-based blocks, or lexical-overlap top-k) — same top-k count; (A3) Random block selection (matched count) — lower-bound control |
| **Unit representations** | (B1) Original learned/derived block representation — reference; (B2) Token-mean pooling (simple alternative); (B3) Shuffled/random unit embeddings (permute representations across blocks within document; and/or Gaussian noise) — lower-bound control |

Crossing A1–A3 × B1–B3 gives 9 cells; the most informative comparisons for **attribution** are:
- **A1B1 vs. A2B1 / A3B1** (hold representations fixed): isolates dynamic lookup.
- **A1B1 vs. A1B2 / A1B3** (hold lookup fixed): isolates representation quality.
- **A1B1 vs. A3B3** (both destroyed): total memory-mechanism contribution.
- Diagonal/mixed cells (e.g., A2B3) test whether the two factors interact (compensatory vs. independent contributions).

**Benchmarks & metrics (candidates):** LongBench suite (NarrativeQA, Qasper, QMSum, Multi-doc QA, HotpotQA, passage retrieval), a needle-in-a-haystack (NIAH) test at multiple depths (esp. *deep*, far-from-window positions — the regime where retrieval matters most), and a streaming/concatenation extrapolation task (e.g., PG-19–style continuous text perplexity at lengths beyond training context). Metrics: task accuracy/EM/F1 per benchmark, retrieval-position-resolved NIAH accuracy curves, streaming perplexity; plus **efficiency** metrics (tokens attended, latency) to verify conditions are matched in cost.

**Controls:**
- Identical token budget and attention compute across conditions (same activation window size, same number of selected blocks per query, same block size).
- Parameter-count control: ablations that change representation computation should use the same backbone weights (frozen, training-free, per InfLLM) so parameter counts are inherently matched; if any variant introduces new parameters, add a parameter-matched variant or evaluation-only (frozen) version.
- Multiple memory capacities (vary top-k ∈ {small, medium, large} and block size) to test whether the ablation gap widens/shrinks with memory budget — a robustness axis, not just a point estimate.
- Same base LLM checkpoint and decoding settings across all cells; ≥3 seeds for stochastic variants (random selection/shuffling); report CIs.
- Sanity checks: full-attention reference upper bound (if compute allows, at shorter lengths) and sliding-window-only baseline (no retrieved memory at all) as an absolute floor.

**Primary readout:** Δ performance of each single-factor ablation relative to A1B1, interpreted against the floor (sliding-window) and ceiling (full attention) to express "criticality" as fraction-of-gap recovered.

## 3) Assumptions (consequential, flagged unverified) and uncertainties

